task: http-post-body-audit
agent: opus5-http
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/http-post-body-audit
worktree: ../okay-wt-post
spec: specs/http.md (the law, to be added) — BACKLOG entry

why: a POST body reaching a route is the thing every REST route needs
  and NOTHING in the tree asserts, on any backend. Jetty's did not
  arrive at all until mcp-push found it live (an MCP route answered
  every message as damaged because every body was empty). The other
  two read it in code today; nobody has run a program that proves it.

what: ONE program that POSTs a body and echoes it back, run against
  all three servers (JDK okay.http.Server, Jetty, Netty) in
  TestBackends — the suite whose whole point is one program, every
  backend. PROVE the net works: revert Jetty's `posted` in the
  worktree, watch the law fail, restore it. Also state in specs/http.md
  what the default gate can and cannot catch here: every real-socket
  suite is Live-tagged by policy (nio-port-scope), so this class of
  regression is caught by `sbt integrationTest`, not `sbt test`.

known before starting: Jetty reads it in `posted` (Jetty.scala, GET
  and HEAD excepted so a WebSocket upgrade is not consumed); Netty
  reads `req.content` after HttpObjectAggregator; okay.http.Server
  reads `x.getRequestBody.readAllBytes()`. Nio.scala is NOT an HTTP
  server (raw TCP) — the BACKLOG entry's "NIO" is a misnomer and the
  entry gets corrected when it closes.
